# ==========================================================================
# kit.config - THE ONE FILE WHERE THIS KIT MEETS YOUR PROJECT
# ==========================================================================
# The annotated registry: every `{{SLOT}}` token that appears in any template
# under modules/ is REGISTERED here and nowhere else. If a template uses a slot
# this file does not define, that is a bug in the kit (see the self-check
# below).
#
# Registered is not the same as valued. Some keys are registered here and get
# their value elsewhere, on purpose: PROJECT_ROOT ships EMPTY and PROTECTED_PATH
# ships as NONE, because an absolute path is wrong for everyone else who clones
# - their values belong in the gitignored `kit.config.local` beside this file,
# or nowhere at all. And nothing reads a config key to FILL a template slot:
# substitution is by hand (see below), so `{{PROJECT_ROOT}}` in the module 02
# settings template is an absolute path you type in at QUICKSTART Step 4,
# whether or not the key here is set.
#
# THE TWO-FILE SPLIT - copy BOTH:
#
#     cp kit.config.example       kit.config          # COMMITTED
#     cp kit.config.local.example kit.config.local    # GITIGNORED
#
#   kit.config        repo-relative, shareable values. It MUST be committed:
#                     a config the tools cannot find is a set of rules that
#                     silently stops existing, and nothing in any output says
#                     so.
#   kit.config.local  absolute paths and the protected location. It must NOT
#                     be committed: those values are wrong for everyone else
#                     who clones, and publish a small map of your machine.
#
# Both are read from the same directory, `.local` last, later wins. The kit
# ships a filled-in `kit.config` of its own, next to this file, as a worked
# example.
#
# HOW SUBSTITUTION HAPPENS: by hand, deliberately, once. There is no templating
# engine, and that is a decision rather than an omission - the templates are
# documents a human has to read and argue with before they bind anything. Open
# a template, replace the slots, delete the guidance comments you have absorbed,
# keep the ones you have not. `kit.config` then survives as the registry of what
# you chose, which is what QUICKSTART.md's final check reads back to you.
#
# FORMAT: KEY = value. `#` starts a comment. No quoting rules, no interpolation.
# Values shown are ILLUSTRATIVE DEFAULTS, not recommendations, except where the
# comment says otherwise.
#
# SELF-CHECK - every slot a template uses must be defined here. Run this after
# any edit to a template, from the kit root; it should print nothing:
#
#   python -c "import re,pathlib;d={m.group(1) for m in re.finditer(r'(?m)^([A-Z0-9_]+)\s*=',pathlib.Path('kit.config.example').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))};u={m.group(1) for p in pathlib.Path('modules').rglob('*') if p.is_file() for m in re.finditer(r'\{\{([A-Z0-9_]+)\}\}',p.read_text(encoding='utf-8',errors='ignore'))};print('\n'.join(sorted(u-d)))"
#
# A template using an undefined slot is a slot the adopter will never fill in,
# and an unfilled slot ships to production looking like a placeholder nobody saw.
# ==========================================================================


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. IDENTITY AND GEOGRAPHY
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Human-readable project name. Appears in governance headers, ledger titles,
# CI job names, and the status board.
PROJECT_NAME = Example Project

# THIS ONE BELONGS IN kit.config.local, OR NOWHERE AT ALL.
#
# It is an absolute path, and this file is the COMMITTED half - putting it here
# contradicts the split described at the top and hands everyone who clones a
# path that is wrong on their machine. Two better options, in order:
#
#   1. OMIT IT. Every tool resolves the root by walking up to `.git` first, and
#      that covers a normal checkout. This is the right answer for most repos.
#   2. Set it in `kit.config.local` for the cases discovery cannot cover - a
#      worktree, a non-git checkout, a harness launched from elsewhere.
#
# If you do set it: forward slashes on Windows, because several tools normalise
# backslashes and several do not, and a path that only matches half the time is
# a tripwire that fires half the time.
# THE SLOT IS NOT THIS KEY. `{{PROJECT_ROOT}}` appears in the module 02
# settings template's three hook commands, and that slot is filled BY HAND at
# QUICKSTART Step 4 with the absolute path to your repo root. Nothing reads
# this key to fill it. Leaving the key empty is the recommended answer and does
# not mean leaving the slot empty: an empty slot yields
# `python "/tools/hook_model_gate.py"`, which the next `--armed` run reports as
# UNSTARTABLE. This key is a RUNTIME override, used only when `.git` discovery
# fails.
#
# Left EMPTY on purpose. Empty means "discover it" - and the key stays
# present, not commented out, so this file remains the complete registry
# of every slot (see the self-check in the header).
PROJECT_ROOT =

# Where durable knowledge lives OUTSIDE the repo - the notes system, wiki, or
# vault that outlives any one session. The sidequest module writes its close
# record here; the governance template points at it for source-of-truth docs.
# Set to NONE if you have no such place. The templates do NOT branch on that
# value - `{{KNOWLEDGE_DIR}}` is interpolated unconditionally - so when it is
# NONE, substitute a REPO path (`docs`) at the two sites that use it rather
# than the literal word: `modules/01-governance/CLAUDE.md.template` and
# `modules/08-collaboration/PROFILE-TEMPLATE.md`. The repo copy then becomes
# source of truth instead of a mirror; say so where the template says it is not.
#
# WHICH HALF: a repo-relative value (`docs`) is shareable and belongs here, in
# the committed file. An ABSOLUTE path does not - set it in kit.config.local
# instead and leave this key alone, or the committed config carries one
# machine's geography and the de-identification scan reports it as an escape.
# The overlay is read last, so the key is still visible to everything that
# reads it.
KNOWLEDGE_DIR = /abs/path/to/your/knowledge-base

# Directory (repo-relative) where stage reports are saved verbatim by the
# coordinator. Lane agents never write here - they return report bodies.
REPORTS_DIR = docs/reports

# Directory (repo-relative) holding the four ledgers from modules/04-ledgers.
LEDGERS_DIR = docs

# Repo-relative path to the current checkpoint/resume document, or a glob whose
# newest match is the one to read. This is the first thing a cold session reads.
CHECKPOINT_GLOB = docs/CHECKPOINT-*.md


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. MODEL TIERS
#    The kit is written for a harness where a spawn can name its model. Names
#    are yours; the kit only cares that the three roles are DISTINCT and that
#    the orchestrator tier is never the default for lane work.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The seat that charters, launches, synthesises, commits, and talks to the
# owner. It must never be the tier that executes lane work.
ORCHESTRATOR_TIER = your-top-tier-model

# Implementers, reviewers, smoke/floors engineers, investigators.
LANE_TIER = your-mid-tier-model

# Mechanical sweeps: renames, inventories, format passes.
SWEEP_TIER = your-small-model

# Agent types that carry their own model definition and are therefore exempt
# from the "declare a tier" rule. Comma-separated. Keep this list SHORT and
# audited - every entry is a hole in the gate.
MODEL_EXEMPT_TYPES = fork

# Named so charters can forbid it explicitly: no spawn, at any depth, may
# request the orchestrator tier by name. Usually the same value as
# ORCHESTRATOR_TIER; it is a separate slot because some harnesses expose the
# orchestrator under a different identifier than the one you would type.
FORBIDDEN_SPAWN_TIER = your-top-tier-model


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. CERTIFICATION
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# THE one command that certifies a tree. One invocation, one summary line, one
# exit code. If you need two commands, you do not yet have certification - you
# have a habit.
GATE_COMMAND = python tools/verify.py

# Repo-relative path to the verify runner itself (module 03 gives you a
# skeleton to copy here).
VERIFY_RUNNER = tools/verify.py

# THE JUDGE SURFACE - paths whose contents decide what "green" MEANS. An
# uncommitted edit to any of them invalidates certification, because the run
# would be judging a tree by rules that are not in the tree. Comma-separated,
# repo-relative.
#
# NAME FILES, NOT DIRECTORIES. `tools` is the tempting value and it is almost
# always wrong: a whole tools directory sweeps in scratch scripts, one-off
# migration helpers, formatting passes and other cosmetics, so certification
# starts going red for reasons that have nothing to do with judgement. What
# happens next is predictable - people stop reading the gate, then they start
# working around it, and a control everybody routes around enforces nothing
# while still costing everyone attention. List the actual judges.
#
# AND LIST THIS FILE. **The config that parameterises the judges is itself a
# judge.** Measured on this kit: with `kit.config` missing from the list, an
# uncommitted `FORBIDDEN_SPAWN_TIER = nothing-is-forbidden` produced a clean
# `VERIFY: PASS` and a hooks gate still reporting that the rules "decide
# correctly" - because the fixture guarding that rule reads its expectation
# from the very file being weakened. Fixture and rule move together, so the
# fixture is blind to it.
#
# WHO READS THIS KEY: nothing does. The judges gate reads the `JUDGE_PATHS`
# constant inside your copy of `verify.py`. This line is documentation, and
# keeping the two in agreement is a manual job - see QUICKSTART Step 4.
JUDGE_PATHS = tools/verify.py, tools/hook_model_gate.py, tools/hook_fixtures.py, .claude/settings.json, kit.config

# THE CERTIFIED TREE - the paths the gates are actually about. Comma-separated,
# repo-relative. Deliberately narrower than the whole repo: a docs commit must
# not invalidate a certification, or nobody will keep the token honest.
#
# WHO READS THIS KEY: `hook_model_gate.py`, and only for the cert-green
# tripwire. The judges gate reads the `CERT_PATHS` constant inside your copy of
# `verify.py`. Keep the two in agreement by hand: if they disagree, the hook and
# the runner hold different opinions about what has been certified, and neither
# will say so.
CERT_PATHS = src, tests, assets

# Toolchain pin. The gate asserts the version string exactly; "it built on my
# machine" is a measurement of the machine.
TOOLCHAIN_NAME = your-runtime
TOOLCHAIN_VERSION_CMD = your-runtime --version
TOOLCHAIN_PIN = 1.2.3
PYTHON_MIN = 3.10

# Where the verify runner writes logs and artefacts. MUST be outside
# PROJECT_ROOT: a run that writes into the tree it judges dirties that tree.
VERIFY_OUT_DIR = /abs/path/outside/the/repo/verify-out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. THE OPTIONAL PROTECTED-PATH TRIPWIRE
#    Turn this on when there is a location agents must not modify without a
#    human nod: a deployed build, a client-facing export, a production config,
#    a data directory. Leave DISABLED and the hook simply never consults it.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

PROTECTED_PATH_ENABLED = false

# Absolute path (or distinctive fragment). The hook matches it in file paths
# and in shell command strings, after normalising backslashes to slashes.
#
# ALSO A kit.config.local VALUE. `PROTECTED_PATH_ENABLED` above is a shareable
# boolean and belongs here; the PATH is one machine's geography and does not.
# Splitting them is not fussiness - a committed absolute path is what makes the
# de-identification scan in QUICKSTART Step 9 unreachable by its own advice.
# NONE here; the real value goes in kit.config.local when you enable the
# tripwire. The key stays present so the registry stays complete.
PROTECTED_PATH = NONE

# The pre-authorisation token. When certification is fully green the
# coordinator writes {"sha": "<commit>"} here, and the tripwire ALLOWS instead
# of ASKING - but only while no commit touching CERT_PATHS has landed since,
# and only while CERT_PATHS is clean in `git status`. Certification is a
# property of a TREE, not of a commit.
CERT_TOKEN_FILE = .claude/cert-green.json


# How the harness invokes an interpreter. Used in the settings template's hook
# commands. Give an absolute path if your harness does not inherit your PATH -
# a hook that cannot start is a hook that silently stops guarding.
PYTHON_BIN = python


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. STATUS BOARD
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The full command the harness runs to render the status line. Substituted
# into the settings template. Set to NONE and delete that block to skip
# module 05.
#
# Defaults to the PORTABLE board, which is the one module 05 recommends -
# stock Python, runs on any host. The pwsh variant is the Windows-optimised
# alternative and is commented out below. Use an ABSOLUTE path: the harness
# does not promise a working directory, and a status line that fails to start
# produces no board at all, which reads exactly like "nothing is happening".
# SB-B. This is an ABSOLUTE-PATH key, so like PROJECT_ROOT and PROTECTED_PATH
# it belongs in `kit.config.local`, not in the committed half. The worked
# example lives there.
#
# TWO RULES WHEN YOU WRITE IT, both learned the hard way:
#   * NO DOUBLE QUOTES. This value is substituted into a JSON string
#     (`"command": "{{STATUSLINE_CMD}}"`), so an embedded `"` produces a
#     settings file that does not parse - and a harness that cannot parse its
#     settings has no hooks either, which turns a cosmetic mistake into a
#     silently disarmed gate.
#   * If the path contains spaces, wrap it in SINGLE quotes. They survive JSON
#     substitution untouched and both pwsh and POSIX shells accept them.
STATUSLINE_CMD = NONE

# Context-window bar: cells, and the cell index of the "clear the session" mark.
STATUS_BAR_CELLS = 16
STATUS_CLEAR_MARK_PCT = 75

# Sidequest banner staleness. Past this many days the banner turns amber: an
# open side quest that has stopped being a detour and become the main line is a
# fact the board should state, not a fact you should notice.
SIDEQUEST_STALE_DAYS = 3

# Repo-relative flag file whose EXISTENCE means "this session is forked".
SIDEQUEST_FLAG = .claude/sidequest.json

# Optional single-line file the coordinator hand-maintains for stage state.
# Set to NONE to drop the segment.
STATUS_BOARD_LINE_FILE = /abs/path/to/statusboard.txt

# Harness-specific: where per-subagent transcripts live, if your harness exposes
# them. Used only to render "who is flying". Set to NONE to drop the segment.
AGENT_TRANSCRIPT_DIR = NONE

# The escape-rate segment: OPT-IN, and NONE by default.
#
# Name the ledger that carries your escape-rate table - the same file module
# 03's `escapes` gate reads - and the board renders the rate `escape_rate.py`
# computes from it, plus one tick per round scaled against the ceiling. Leave
# it NONE and the segment does not exist: no marker, no file read, not even the
# import of the tool. Module 05's README explains why this one is offered
# rather than switched on, and what the board shows when the ledger aborts.
#
# Repo-relative resolves against PROJECT_ROOT, not the working directory. The
# board needs `escape_rate.py` beside it in `tools/`, or the kit's own
# `modules/04-ledgers/` layout; if it finds neither, the segment says
# UNAVAILABLE rather than disappearing.
STATUS_ESCAPE_LEDGER = NONE


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. CI
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

CI_RUNNER_OS = windows-latest
CI_TIMEOUT_MINUTES = 90

# Gates the hosted runner cannot host. Comma-separated; every entry needs a
# REASON in the workflow header, and the reasons must be different sentences -
# collapsing them into "not supported yet" loses the only useful information,
# which is which of them can ever be lifted.
CI_SKIP_GATES = smoke

# Exit code the CI contract step requires. 3 (PARTIAL) when CI_SKIP_GATES is
# non-empty; 0 (PASS) only when CI runs every gate.
CI_EXPECTED_EXIT = 3

# Pinned toolchain download for the runner.
CI_TOOLCHAIN_URL = https://example.invalid/your-runtime-1.2.3.zip
CI_TOOLCHAIN_ARCHIVE = your-runtime-1.2.3.zip
CI_TOOLCHAIN_SHA512 = <paste the checksum from the publisher's own sums file>
CI_ARTIFACT_RETENTION_DAYS = 14


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. COST DISCIPLINE
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# process / implementation ratio ceiling. DERIVE this from your own first few
# stages rather than adopting a number; the ledger template shows the
# derivation. A ceiling is a tripwire that opens a conversation at a gate,
# never an automatic budget.
RATIO_CEILING = derive-from-your-own-data

# Stages without a fired rule before that rule faces demotion review.
DEMOTION_REVIEW_STAGES = 3


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. VOCABULARY
#    The kit refers to the human seat generically. If your project has a
#    different word for it (client, PO, principal, maintainer), set it once.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

OWNER_ROLE = the owner
COORDINATOR_ROLE = the coordinator


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. WRITING STANDARD
#    The voice used for everything that gets committed or published: stage
#    reports, checkpoints, papers, module docs. This exists because the
#    reference build's owner had to ask for plain prose repeatedly before it
#    became a rule - a repeated correction is a standing standard.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `technical`  - plain technical-writer prose: short declarative sentences,
#                defined terms, active voice, tables where they help, no
#                aphoristic flourishes. The default, and the right choice for
#                anything a newcomer will read cold.
# `doctrinal`  - the compressed, aphoristic house style some operators prefer
#                for internal doctrine. Legible to insiders; harder for
#                everyone else. Choose it deliberately, per project.
# The slot is consumed by module 01's governance template (a WRITING STANDARD
# line) and by humans; no tool parses it. Conversation with the owner is not
# bound by it - only artifacts that outlive the session are.
PROSE_VOICE = technical
